 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "INVERNESS ICE RINK. Home to curling, skating and one Keeper who will not be hurried. Curling is 'the roaring game' because of the sound the granite stones make on the ice. Sweeping is allowed. Shouting is encouraged."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE VICTORIAN MARKET. Built in 1870 and rebuilt after a fire in 1890, with a wrought iron roof that still keeps the rain off the tablet. Look for the tablet. Scottish tablet is sugar, butter and condensed milk, and it will try to buy you a new filling."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE CAMANACHD PITCH. Shinty is the Highland game, a cousin of Irish hurling, played with a stick called a caman (the same word as 'crooked') and a ball. Two teams of twelve. Everyone swings. Everyone pretends it was an accident."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "THE MORAY FIRTH. The sea off Inverness is home to Britain's biggest pod of bottlenose dolphins, about 200 of them. They're the largest of their kind in the world, and they jump for fun. The ones at Chanonry Point do it at high tide, in front of a crowd with cameras."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "THE RIVER NESS (Abhainn Nis). Only about 10 km long, which makes it one of the shortest rivers in Britain, but it carries all of Loch Ness down to the sea. The town's Gaelic name, Inbhir Nis, means 'mouth of the Ness'."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE NESS ISLANDS. A park on a string of river islands, linked by Victorian footbridges since 1853. The trees are huge. The bridges wobble gently, as a hint."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "INVERNESS CASTLE. The pink sandstone building you see was built in the 1830s as a courthouse and jail, on a site that has had a fortress for nearly a thousand years. The statue is Flora MacDonald, who helped Prince Charles Edward Stuart escape in 1746."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "ST ANDREW'S CATHEDRAL. Built in the 1860s, it was the first new cathedral in Britain since the Reformation. The two towers were added later, which is why one of them is a bit shy."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "INBHIR NIS \u00b7 INVERNESS. 'Capital of the Highlands' since 565 AD, when St Columba came to visit the Pictish king Brude and had a word about a monster on the way. F\u00e0ilte."
  }
 ],
